[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What validation rules does the shared layer need to cover beyond "required field"? → Required + common formats[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Second question — this one changes the interface every form will be written against, so here's the fork before the
  options:

  [1mThe fork:[0m what [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m hands back. Today it returns one string for the whole form ([38;5;153m{valid: false, error: "Missing [39m
  [38;5;153mrequired fields"}[39m), so a form can only say "something's wrong." A per-field map lets each input show its own message
  next to it.

  - [1mSingle message (status quo shape)[0m — cheapest, zero change to the existing login handler, but a 6-field signup form
    can only show one error at a time and can't highlight which input. Changing this later means rewriting every call
    site.
  - [1mPer-field map[0m ([38;5;153m{valid, errors: {username: "Username is required"}}[39m) — each form decides whether to show one message
    or all of them; the single-message behavior is still reachable by taking the first entry. Slightly more code in the
    login handler today.
  - [1mPer-field map + DOM rendering helper[0m — also owns inserting/clearing error text in the page, so forms don't each
    hand-roll it. Buys consistency, but couples the validation module to a specific HTML structure, which is the part
    that's expensive to undo.

  [1mRecommendation: per-field map, no DOM helper.[0m It's the choice that's hard to reverse (it's the interface), and a plain
  data result keeps the module testable without a DOM. Rendering is easy to add later once two real forms show what
  they actually need.
